# aether-string-interpolation-syntax

Aether interpolated strings use the `echo"..."` (or `f"..."`) prefix with `$~variable` for wave-variable injection and `${expression}` for arbitrary expression injection; raw strings use `r"..."`.

## Procedures
- 1. Plain Echo (string) literal: `"literal text"` with C-style escape sequences (`\n`, `\t`, `\\`).
- 2. Interpolated Echo: prefix with `echo` or `f`: `echo"Hello, $~name!"`.
- 3. Inject a wave variable: `$~variableName` inside an interpolated Echo (note: `~` is part of the identifier).
- 4. Inject an arbitrary expression: `${expression}` — e.g., `echo"Result: ${~a + ~b}"`.
- 5. Raw Echo (no escape processing): prefix with `r`: `r"raw\nstring"`.
- 6. Bytes literal: prefix with `b` or `B`; ASCII-only content.

## Conventions
- Both `echo"..."` and `f"..."` prefixes produce interpolated Echoes — they are equivalent.
- When injecting a Wave variable directly, use `$~varName` (tilde is part of the name, not a separate sigil).
- Use `${...}` for any expression that is not a simple variable reference.

